IMPORT prelude

-- Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 (NSW) -- Chapter 5, the award of damages, from the seat of the injured person
-- (or the relative claiming for a death).
--
-- SEAT: the injured person, the claimant. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Can I get damages for
-- pain and suffering, and how much at most?", "What earnings count?", "What discount applies?", "What is taken off?",
-- "Must I be found contributorily negligent?", "What do I get after the reduction?", "Can the defendant say I accepted
-- the risk?", "What can I get for care my family gives me?", "Do I get interest?", "Can I get this kind of damages at
-- all?". The court and the insurer get no seat.
--
-- Provisions: ss 122, 123 (application), 125, 126 (in a comment), 127, 130 (economic loss), 131-134 (non-economic
-- loss), 136 (in a comment), 137 (interest), 138 (contributory negligence), 140 (voluntary assumption of risk), 141A,
-- 141B, 141C, 142, 144 (kinds of damages), with ss 83(5), (6) and 84A(3), (4) (payments by the insurer and
-- contributory negligence). The amounts the Authority indexes under s 146 are inputs: the orders are not deposited.
--
-- ANSWERS. As in mac-claims.l4: JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no, NOTHING (with a NOTHING-kind
-- comment) where it does not answer. Amounts are in dollars; percentages are numbers (12 means 12%).
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 16 June 2022; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Motor Accidents Compensation Act 1999 -- damages`

§§ `The nouns`

-- The heads of damages s 137 treats differently.
DECLARE `The damages the interest would be on` IS ONE OF
    `damages for non-economic loss`
    `compensation for gratuitous attendant care services under s 141B`
    `another head of damages`

-- The person's position in a vehicle engaged in motor racing (s 140(2)).
DECLARE `My position in the racing vehicle` IS ONE OF
    `I was its driver`
    `I was a passenger in it`
    `I was not in it`

-- The kinds of damages ss 141A, 141C, 142 and 144 deal with.
DECLARE `The kind of damages I am asking about` IS ONE OF
    `exemplary or punitive damages`
    `damages for the loss of another person's services`
    `damages for treatment and care needs while I am a Lifetime Care and Support participant`
    `compensation for respite care`

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` a `and` b MEANS IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`not below zero` n MEANS IF n LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE n

-- ss 122(1), 123(1): Chapter 5 applies to an award of damages for death or injury caused by the fault of the owner or
-- driver of a motor vehicle in its use or operation, and "A court cannot award damages to a person in respect of a motor
-- accident contrary to this Chapter". s 122(3): it binds the Commission's assessment too. s 123(3): an award contrary to
-- the Chapter need not be paid, and an overpayment can be recovered. Whether the scheme applies at all is asked in
-- mac-claims.l4 (`Does the 1999 scheme cover my claim`).

§§ `Part 5.3 -- non-economic loss (ss 131-134)`

-- s 131 "No damages may be awarded for non-economic loss unless the degree of permanent impairment of the injured person as
-- a result of the injury caused by the motor accident is greater than 10%." s 132(1) "If there is a dispute about whether
-- the degree of permanent impairment of an injured person is sufficient ..., the court may not award any such damages
-- unless the degree of permanent impairment has been assessed by a medical assessor under Part 3.4". s 133: assessed
-- under the Motor Accidents Medical Guidelines (or AMA Guides 4th edition). s 3 "non-economic loss means (a) pain and
-- suffering, (b) loss of amenities of life, (c) loss of expectation of life, (d) disfigurement."
@export Can I be awarded damages for non-economic loss (pain and suffering, loss of amenities and expectation of life, disfigurement) for my motor accident injury (ss 131, 132)?
GIVEN `my degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent impairment from the injury caused by the accident, as a percentage of whole person impairment (for example 12 for 12%), assessed under the Motor Accidents Medical Guidelines
      `the threshold is disputed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a dispute about whether your degree of permanent impairment is enough (more than 10%)
      `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has assessed your degree of permanent impairment under Part 3.4 of the Act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be awarded damages for non-economic loss` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the threshold is disputed` AND (NOT `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment`) THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 132(1) "may not ... unless"
           IF `my degree of permanent impairment` GREATER THAN 10 THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- s 131 "No damages ... unless ... greater than 10%"

-- s 134(1) "The maximum amount that a court may award for non-economic loss is $284,000." s 134(2): if indexed under
-- s 146, "the applicable maximum amount is the amount as at the date the award is made". The s 146 orders are not
-- deposited: the indexed amount is asked. Below the threshold the most is nothing.
@export What is the most I can be awarded for non-economic loss (ss 131, 132, 134)?
GIVEN `my degree of permanent impairment` IS A NUMBER @desc Your degree of permanent impairment from the injury, as a percentage of whole person impairment (for example 12 for 12%)
      `the threshold is disputed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is a dispute about whether your degree of permanent impairment is more than 10%
      `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a medical assessor has assessed your degree of permanent impairment under Part 3.4
      `the maximum at the date of the award` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum for non-economic loss at the date the award is made, in dollars: $284,000 under section 134(1), as indexed each year by the Authority's order under section 146
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most I can get for non-economic loss` MEANS
    IF `Can I be awarded damages for non-economic loss` `my degree of permanent impairment` `the threshold is disputed` `a medical assessor has assessed my impairment` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN `the maximum at the date of the award`
    ELSE 0

§§ `Part 5.2 -- economic loss (ss 125-130)`

-- s 125(1), (2): for damages for past or future loss of earnings or of earning capacity, or for loss of expectation of
-- financial support, "the court is to disregard the amount (if any) by which the injured or deceased person's net weekly
-- earnings would (but for the injury or death) have exceeded $2,500" (indexed under s 146; asked). s 126: future
-- economic loss only on assumptions that accord with the claimant's most likely future circumstances, adjusted for the
-- percentage possibility of those events; that is the court's judgment and is not encoded.
@export What weekly earnings can be counted when my damages for loss of earnings or earning capacity (or a family's loss of financial support) are worked out (s 125)?
GIVEN `the net weekly earnings but for the accident` IS A NUMBER @desc The net weekly earnings, in dollars, that you (or the person who died) would have had but for the injury or death
      `the weekly maximum in force` IS A NUMBER @desc The weekly maximum, in dollars: $2,500 under section 125(2), as indexed each year by the Authority's order under section 146
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What weekly earnings are counted` MEANS
    `the lesser of` `the net weekly earnings but for the accident` `and` `the weekly maximum in force`

-- s 127(1), (2): a lump sum for future economic loss (earning capacity, financial support, future domestic, nursing and
-- attendance services, future expenditure) is discounted to present value at "the prescribed discount rate", which is
-- "(a) a discount rate of the percentage prescribed by the regulations, or (b) if no percentage is so prescribed--a
-- discount rate of 5%". The regulations are not deposited: whether they prescribe a percentage is asked.
@export At what discount rate is the lump sum for my future economic loss brought to present value (s 127)?
GIVEN `the regulations prescribe a percentage` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulations under the Act prescribe a discount rate percentage
      `the prescribed percentage` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage the regulations prescribe (for example 5 for 5%); any number if none is prescribed
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What discount rate applies` MEANS
    IF `the regulations prescribe a percentage` THEN `the prescribed percentage` ELSE 5

-- s 130 "A court must reduce the amount of economic loss ... by-- (a) the amount of any entitlement to or payment of
-- compensation for expenses under the Victims Compensation Act 1996 for the injury suffered in the accident, and (b)
-- payments made to or on behalf of the claimant by an insurer or Nominal Defendant in relation to a claim made by the
-- claimant (including payments made under Part 3.2 or Part 4.3), and (c) any other amount of a kind prescribed by the
-- regulations". The reduction stops at nothing: economic loss is not made negative (fork F10).
@export By how much is my economic loss reduced for payments I have had or am entitled to, and what is left (s 130)?
GIVEN `my economic loss` IS A NUMBER @desc Your economic loss from the accident as assessed before this reduction, in dollars
      `victims compensation for expenses` IS A NUMBER @desc Any compensation for expenses you were entitled to or were paid under the Victims Compensation Act 1996 for the injury, in dollars
      `payments by the insurer or Nominal Defendant` IS A NUMBER @desc Payments made to you or for you by the insurer or the Nominal Defendant on your claim, including early payments (Part 3.2) and payments for treatment, rehabilitation, care and financial hardship (Part 4.3), in dollars
      `other prescribed amounts` IS A NUMBER @desc Any other amount of a kind prescribed by the regulations for section 130, in dollars (0 if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What economic loss is left after the s 130 reduction` MEANS
    `not below zero` (`my economic loss` MINUS (`victims compensation for expenses` PLUS `payments by the insurer or Nominal Defendant` PLUS `other prescribed amounts`))

§§ `Part 5.4 -- contributory negligence and voluntary assumption of risk (ss 138, 140)`

-- s 138(2) "A finding of contributory negligence must be made in the following cases-- (a) where the injured person or
-- deceased person has been convicted of an alcohol or other drug-related offence in relation to the motor accident,
-- unless the plaintiff satisfies the court that the alcohol or other drug involved ... did not contribute in any way to
-- the accident, (b) where-- (i) the injured person (not being a minor) or the deceased person was ... a voluntary
-- passenger ..., and (ii) the driver's ability to drive ... was impaired as a consequence of the consumption of alcohol
-- or any other drug and the injured person or the deceased person was aware, or ought to have been aware, of the
-- impairment, unless ... [they] could not reasonably be expected to have declined to become a passenger ..., (c) where
-- the injured person (not being a minor) or the deceased person was ... not wearing a seat belt when required by law to
-- do so, (d) where the injured person or the deceased person was ... not wearing a protective helmet when required by law
-- to do so." s 138(5): a deceased person is taken to have been convicted where, but for death, they would have been.
-- s 138(7) lists the alcohol or drug-related offences. s 138(6): other grounds remain, so where no case applies the Act
-- does not answer (NOTHING): the common law decides (s 138(1)).
@export Must the court find me (or the person who died) contributorily negligent because of alcohol or drugs, a drunk driver, a seat belt or a helmet (s 138(2))?
GIVEN `I was convicted of an alcohol or drug-related offence for the accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you (or the person who died) were convicted of, or but for death would have been convicted of, an alcohol or other drug-related driving offence in relation to the accident (for example drink-driving, driving under the influence, or refusing a breath test)
      `the alcohol or drug did not contribute in any way` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you can satisfy the court that the alcohol or other drug involved in that offence did not contribute in any way to the accident
      `I was a minor at the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18 at the time of the accident (for the person who died, answer no)
      `I was a voluntary passenger with an impaired driver I knew or ought to have known about` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were a voluntary passenger in or on a vehicle whose driver's ability to drive was impaired by alcohol or another drug, and you were aware, or ought to have been aware, of the impairment
      `I could not reasonably have been expected to decline to be a passenger` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, in the circumstances, you could not reasonably have been expected to decline to become a passenger
      `I was not wearing a seat belt when required by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not wearing a seat belt at the time of the accident when the law required you to
      `I was not wearing a protective helmet when required by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were not wearing a protective helmet at the time of the accident when the law required you to (for example on a motorcycle or bicycle)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must a finding of contributory negligence be made` MEANS
    IF     `case (a)` OR `case (b)` OR `case (c)` OR `I was not wearing a protective helmet when required by law`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 138(6): other grounds remain; the common law decides, s 138(1))
    WHERE
        `case (a)` MEANS `I was convicted of an alcohol or drug-related offence for the accident` AND (NOT `the alcohol or drug did not contribute in any way`)
        `case (b)` MEANS (NOT `I was a minor at the time`) AND `I was a voluntary passenger with an impaired driver I knew or ought to have known about` AND (NOT `I could not reasonably have been expected to decline to be a passenger`)
        `case (c)` MEANS (NOT `I was a minor at the time`) AND `I was not wearing a seat belt when required by law`

-- s 138(3) "The damages recoverable in respect of the motor accident are to be reduced by such percentage as the court
-- thinks just and equitable" (the court's percentage, asked). ss 83(6), 84A(4): the insurer's payments for treatment,
-- rehabilitation, care and financial hardship "are to be included in the damages recoverable ... for the purposes of any
-- reduction of those damages by reason of the contributory negligence". ss 83(5), 84A(3): those payments, made before
-- judgment, are "to the extent of [their] amount, a defence to proceedings" for damages. So the reduction is applied to
-- the whole including the payments, and the payments are then taken off. What is left does not go below nothing (fork
-- F10).
@export After the reduction for my contributory negligence, how much of my damages is still to be paid to me (ss 83(5), (6), 84A(3), (4), 138(3))?
GIVEN `my damages including the insurer's payments` IS A NUMBER @desc Your total damages before any reduction for contributory negligence, in dollars, counting the payments the insurer has already made under sections 83 and 84A
      `the insurer's payments under ss 83 and 84A` IS A NUMBER @desc The payments the insurer has already made to you or for you under section 83 (treatment, rehabilitation, respite and attendant care) and section 84A (financial hardship), in dollars
      `the percentage reduction` IS A NUMBER @desc The percentage by which the court reduces your damages for contributory negligence (for example 25 for 25%)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much is still to be paid after contributory negligence` MEANS
    `not below zero` (`the reduced damages` MINUS `the insurer's payments under ss 83 and 84A`)
    WHERE
        `the reduced damages` MEANS `my damages including the insurer's payments` MINUS ((`my damages including the insurer's payments` TIMES `the percentage reduction`) DIVIDED BY 100)

-- s 140(1) "Except as provided by subsection (2), the defence of volenti non fit injuria is not available in proceedings
-- for damages arising from a motor accident but, where that defence would otherwise have been available, the amount of
-- any damages is to be reduced to such extent as is just and equitable on the presumption that the injured person or
-- deceased person was negligent". s 140(2): it is available where the accident occurs while the vehicle is engaged in
-- motor racing (an organised motor sports event, or an offence under s 115 of the Road Transport Act 2013, s 140(3)),
-- against its driver, or a passenger "other than a passenger who is less than 18 years of age or who otherwise lacked
-- capacity to consent to be a voluntary passenger". s 141: the driver's standard of care is not lowered by what the
-- passenger knew of the driver's skill or experience.
@export Can the defendant defeat my claim by saying I voluntarily accepted the risk (volenti non fit injuria) (s 140)?
GIVEN `the vehicle was engaged in motor racing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the accident happened while the vehicle was taking part in an organised motor sports event, or in racing, a speed attempt or a similar activity that is an offence under section 115 of the Road Transport Act 2013
      `my position` IS A `My position in the racing vehicle` @desc Your position. One of: "I was its driver", "I was a passenger in it", "I was not in it"
      `I was under 18 or lacked capacity to consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were under 18, or otherwise lacked capacity to consent to be a voluntary passenger
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the defendant rely on voluntary assumption of risk` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the vehicle was engaged in motor racing`)
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 140(1) "is not available" (damages may instead be reduced as for negligence)
    ELSE CONSIDER `my position`
         WHEN `I was its driver` THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 140(2)(a)
         WHEN `I was a passenger in it` THEN
             IF `I was under 18 or lacked capacity to consent`
             THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 140(2)(b) "other than"
             ELSE JUST TRUE
         OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- s 140(1)

§§ `Part 5.4 -- care, services and the kinds of damages (ss 141A-144)`

-- s 141B(1) compensation for gratuitous attendant care services (provided by another person, not paid for and not
-- payable) "must not exceed the amount determined in accordance with this section". (2) "No compensation is to be
-- awarded if the services would have been provided to the person even if the person had not been injured". (3) "no
-- compensation is to be awarded unless the services are provided (or to be provided)-- (a) for at least 6 hours per
-- week, and (b) for a period of at least 6 consecutive months." (4) at 40 hours a week or more, the most is a week's
-- average weekly total earnings of all employees in NSW (the Australian Statistician's estimate; asked); (5) under 40
-- hours, an hourly rate of one-fortieth of that. s 141A(1): no damages at all for a Lifetime Care and Support Scheme
-- participant's treatment and care needs relating to the injury during participation (attendant care is a treatment and
-- care need under that Act, which is not deposited: fork F11). Where the person paid or must pay for the care, s 141B does
-- not apply and the Act sets no cap (NOTHING).
@export What is the most I can be awarded per week for attendant care that family or friends give me free of charge (ss 141A, 141B)?
GIVEN `I paid or must pay for the care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have paid, or are liable to pay, for the attendant care services
      `I am a Lifetime Care and Support participant for this injury` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a participant in the Lifetime Care and Support Scheme (Motor Accidents (Lifetime Care and Support) Act 2006) for this injury, for the period of the care
      `the care would have been given even without the accident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the services would have been provided to you even if you had not been injured in the accident
      `hours of care per week` IS A NUMBER @desc How many hours a week the attendant care services are (or will be) provided
      `consecutive months of care` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many consecutive months the services are (or will be) provided
      `average weekly total earnings in NSW` IS A NUMBER @desc The Australian Statistician's estimate of the average weekly total earnings of all employees in New South Wales for the relevant quarter, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most per week for gratuitous attendant care` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I paid or must pay for the care` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 141B covers only care not paid for; the text sets no cap)
           IF `I am a Lifetime Care and Support participant for this injury` THEN JUST 0  -- s 141A(1) (fork F11)
           IF `the care would have been given even without the accident` THEN JUST 0  -- s 141B(2)
           IF `hours of care per week` LESS THAN 6 THEN JUST 0  -- s 141B(3)(a)
           IF `consecutive months of care` LESS THAN 6 THEN JUST 0  -- s 141B(3)(b)
           IF `hours of care per week` AT LEAST 40 THEN JUST `average weekly total earnings in NSW`  -- s 141B(4)
           OTHERWISE JUST ((`average weekly total earnings in NSW` DIVIDED BY 40) TIMES `hours of care per week`)  -- s 141B(5)

-- s 144 "A court cannot award exemplary or punitive damages to a person in respect of a motor accident." s 142(1) "No
-- damages for the loss of the services of a person are to be awarded in respect of a motor accident", except (s 142(2))
-- in an action under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 (with s 141B(3)-(7) applied to attendant care, s 142(3)).
-- s 141A(1) "No damages may be awarded to a person who is a participant in the Scheme ... in respect of any of the
-- treatment and care needs of the participant ... that relate to the motor accident injury ... and that arise during the
-- period in which the person is a participant". s 141C "An award of damages may include compensation for reasonable and
-- necessary respite care in respect of a claimant who is seriously injured and in need of constant care over a long term"
-- (a grant limited in its own words: outside it, NOTHING).
@export Can damages of this kind be awarded to me for my motor accident (ss 141A, 141C, 142, 144)?
GIVEN `the kind of damages` IS A `The kind of damages I am asking about` @desc The kind of damages. One of: "exemplary or punitive damages", "damages for the loss of another person's services", "damages for treatment and care needs while I am a Lifetime Care and Support participant", "compensation for respite care"
      `the action is under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is an action for a death under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897
      `I am seriously injured and need constant care over a long term` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are seriously injured and in need of constant care over a long term
      `the respite care is reasonable and necessary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respite care is reasonable and necessary
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can damages of this kind be awarded` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of damages`
    WHEN `exemplary or punitive damages` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 144
    WHEN `damages for the loss of another person's services` THEN
        IF `the action is under the Compensation to Relatives Act 1897`
        THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 142(2): the bar does not apply; the 1897 Act and the general law decide)
        ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 142(1)
    WHEN `damages for treatment and care needs while I am a Lifetime Care and Support participant` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 141A(1)
    OTHERWISE
        IF `I am seriously injured and need constant care over a long term` AND `the respite care is reasonable and necessary`
        THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 141C
        ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited in its own words; the case falls outside it)

§§ `s 137 -- interest`

-- s 137(1) "A plaintiff has only such right to interest on damages ... as is conferred by this section." (2) none on
-- s 141B compensation; (3) none on non-economic loss. (4)(a) on other damages, "Interest is not payable ... unless-- (i)
-- information that would enable a proper assessment of the plaintiff's claim has been given to the defendant and the
-- defendant has had a reasonable opportunity to make an offer of settlement ... but has not made such an offer, or (ii)
-- the defendant has had a reasonable opportunity to make a revised offer ... in the light of further information ... but
-- has not made such an offer, or (iii) if the defendant is insured under a third-party policy or is the Nominal
-- Defendant, the insurer has failed to comply with its duty under section 83, or (iv) if the defendant has made an offer
-- of settlement, the amount of all damages ... awarded by the court (without ... interest) is more than 20% higher than
-- the highest amount offered by the defendant and the highest amount is unreasonable having regard to the information
-- available to the defendant when the offer was made." (b) the highest offer is not unreasonable if the defendant could
-- not then reasonably assess the full entitlement; (c) offers must be in writing. "Reasonable opportunity" and
-- "unreasonable" are the court's judgments, asked.
@export Can I get interest on my damages (s 137(1)-(4))?
GIVEN `the damages` IS A `The damages the interest would be on` @desc Which damages. One of: "damages for non-economic loss", "compensation for gratuitous attendant care services under s 141B", "another head of damages" (for example past loss of earnings or past expenses)
      `the defendant had the information and a reasonable opportunity to offer but made no offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you gave the defendant information enabling a proper assessment of your full entitlement and the defendant had a reasonable opportunity to make a written offer of settlement (where appropriate) but made none
      `the defendant had further information and a reasonable opportunity to revise its offer but did not` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, after further information from you enabling a proper assessment, the defendant had a reasonable opportunity to make a revised written offer (where appropriate) but made none
      `the insurer failed in its s 83 duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant is insured under a third-party policy (or is the Nominal Defendant) and the insurer failed to comply with its duty under section 83 to pay hospital, medical, rehabilitation and care expenses as incurred
      `the defendant made a written offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant made a written offer of settlement
      `the highest written offer` IS A NUMBER @desc The highest amount the defendant offered in writing, in dollars (0 if none)
      `the damages awarded without interest` IS A NUMBER @desc The total of all damages of any kind awarded by the court, without interest, in dollars
      `the highest offer was unreasonable on the information the defendant had` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the highest offer was unreasonable having regard to the information available to the defendant when it was made (it is not unreasonable if the defendant could not then reasonably assess your full entitlement)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I get interest on my damages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the damages`
    WHEN `damages for non-economic loss` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 137(3)
    WHEN `compensation for gratuitous attendant care services under s 141B` THEN JUST FALSE  -- s 137(2)
    OTHERWISE
        IF     `the defendant had the information and a reasonable opportunity to offer but made no offer`
            OR `the defendant had further information and a reasonable opportunity to revise its offer but did not`
            OR `the insurer failed in its s 83 duty`
            OR `case (iv)`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE  -- s 137(4)(a) "not payable ... unless"
    WHERE
        `case (iv)` MEANS
                `the defendant made a written offer`
            AND (`the damages awarded without interest` GREATER THAN ((`the highest written offer` TIMES 120) DIVIDED BY 100))
            AND `the highest offer was unreasonable on the information the defendant had`

-- s 137(6) "The rate of interest to be used in any such calculation is three-quarters of the rate prescribed for the
-- purposes of section 101 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 for the period concerned." (5): from when the loss was first
-- incurred until the court determines the damages. The Civil Procedure Act rate is asked.
@export Where interest is payable on my damages, at what yearly rate is it calculated (s 137(6))?
GIVEN `the Civil Procedure Act s 101 rate` IS A NUMBER @desc The yearly interest rate prescribed for section 101 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 for the period, as a percentage (for example 8 for 8%)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the interest rate on my damages` MEANS (`the Civil Procedure Act s 101 rate` TIMES 3) DIVIDED BY 4
